    {
      "headline": "IMU Meaning in Retail: Definition, Formula and Calculation",
      "url": "https://www.lightspeedhq.com/blog/imu-in-retail/",
      "date": "Sat, 25 Jul 2026 02:15:15 +0000",
      "source": "Lightspeed blog",
      "company": "Lightspeed",
      "type": "public",
      "content_type": "product_release",
      "summary_raw": "IMU stands for Initial Markup, representing the percentage difference between an item&#8217;s wholesale cost and its very first retail price. Nailing this metric right out of the gate gives your business the financial padding to absorb operating expenses and future markdowns without sacrificing profitability. While your final profit margins will inevitably fluctuate, a strong IMU [&hellip;]",
      "emoji": "\ud83d\udcf0"
    },
    {
      "headline": "Retail Replenishment Optimization: Benefits and Best Practices",
      "url": "https://www.lightspeedhq.com/blog/replenishment-optimization/",
      "date": "Sat, 25 Jul 2026 02:07:37 +0000",
      "source": "Lightspeed blog",
      "company": "Lightspeed",
      "type": "public",
      "content_type": "product_release",
      "summary_raw": "When you manage inventory across multiple locations, replenishment optimization is the process of using demand forecasting, real-time analytics and automation to determine the exact timing and quantity for restocking products. It eliminates the guesswork of manual ordering so you can maximize sales while minimizing excess holding costs. Let&#8217;s look at the core formulas that drive [&hellip;]",
      "emoji": "\ud83d\udcf0"
    },
    {
      "headline": "What is Vendor Managed Inventory: Meaning, Process, and Benefits",
      "url": "https://www.lightspeedhq.com/blog/vendor-managed-inventory/",
      "date": "Sat, 25 Jul 2026 01:59:54 +0000",
      "source": "Lightspeed blog",
      "company": "Lightspeed",
      "type": "public",
      "content_type": "product_release",
      "summary_raw": "Managing stock across multiple locations often feels like a constant balancing act between tying up cash and running out of bestsellers. Vendor managed inventory (VMI) is a collaborative supply chain model where the supplier, rather than the buyer, assumes responsibility for monitoring stock levels and automatically replenishing products based on real-time sales data. Handing over [&hellip;]",
      "emoji": "\ud83d\udcf0"
